<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<title>React.js Core - Master Interview Playbook</title>
<style>
  body { font-family: Calibri, 'Segoe UI', Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt; line-height: 1.5; color: #1e293b; margin: 30px; }
  h1 { font-size: 20pt; color: #0284c7; border-bottom: 2px solid #0284c7; padding-bottom: 6px; }
  h2 { font-size: 14pt; color: #0369a1; margin-top: 22px; border-bottom: 1px solid #cbd5e1; padding-bottom: 4px; }
  h3 { font-size: 12pt; color: #0f172a; margin-top: 14px; }
  p, li { margin: 4px 0; }
  .script-box { background: #f0fdf4; border-left: 4px solid #16a34a; padding: 10px 14px; margin: 10px 0; border-radius: 4px; font-style: italic; color: #14532d; }
  .concept-box { background: #eff6ff; border-left: 4px solid #0284c7; padding: 10px 14px; margin: 10px 0; border-radius: 4px; }
  .trap-box { background: #fef2f2; border-left: 4px solid #ef4444; padding: 10px 14px; margin: 10px 0; border-radius: 4px; color: #7f1d1d; }
  .code { font-family: Consolas, 'Courier New', monospace; background: #f1f5f9; padding: 2px 6px; border-radius: 3px; font-size: 10pt; color: #0f172a; }
  pre { background: #0f172a; color: #38bdf8; font-family: Consolas, monospace; padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px; font-size: 10pt; overflow-x: auto; }
  table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 12px 0; }
  th, td { border: 1px solid #cbd5e1; padding: 8px 12px; text-align: left; }
  th { background: #f8fafc; font-weight: bold; color: #1e293b; }
</style>
</head>
<body>

<h1>⚛️ React.js Core & Component Architecture - Master Interview Playbook</h1>
<p><em>Structured with: Concept Clarity + <strong>Exact Speaking Scripts</strong> + Interview Traps & Solutions.</em></p>

<h2>1. React Element vs Component vs Node</h2>
<div class="script-box">
  <strong>🗣️ Speaking Script:</strong><br>
  "Sir, a <strong>React Element</strong> is a lightweight, immutable JavaScript object that describes what should appear on the screen. It is created via JSX or React.createElement(). A <strong>React Component</strong> is a function or class that encapsulates state and logic and returns React Elements. A <strong>React Node</strong> is a broader umbrella term for anything that can be rendered, including React elements, strings, numbers, arrays, or null."
</div>

<h2>2. State vs Props & Immutability Rule</h2>
<div class="script-box">
  <strong>🗣️ Speaking Script:</strong><br>
  "Sir, Props are external inputs passed from parent to child, and they are strictly read-only. State is internal data managed directly within the component that can change over time. We must never mutate state directly because React relies on shallow reference comparison to detect state changes. Mutating an array or object directly preserves the same memory reference, causing React to skip re-rendering."
</div>

<h2>3. Controlled vs Uncontrolled Components</h2>
<div class="script-box">
  <strong>🗣️ Speaking Script:</strong><br>
  "In a <strong>Controlled Component</strong>, the form input's value is driven by React state using value and onChange, making React the single source of truth. In an <strong>Uncontrolled Component</strong>, form data is handled directly by the DOM itself, and we pull values using a ref when needed."
</div>

<h2>4. useRef Hook: The Secret Spy of React (PDF Q14)</h2>
<div class="concept-box">
  <strong>🧠 Dimag Me Samajhne Ke Liye:</strong>
  <span class="code">useRef</span> returns <span class="code">{ current: value }</span>. It has two jobs: (1) Direct DOM access (focus, scroll), (2) Holding a mutable value across renders <strong>WITHOUT triggering a re-render</strong> (e.g., storing <span class="code">setInterval</span> timer ID).
</div>

<h3>useState vs useRef vs Regular Variable (let)</h3>
<table>
  <tr>
    <th>Feature</th>
    <th>useState</th>
    <th>useRef</th>
    <th>Regular let</th>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Triggers Re-render?</strong></td>
    <td><strong>YES</strong></td>
    <td><strong>NO</strong></td>
    <td><strong>NO</strong></td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Persists across renders?</strong></td>
    <td><strong>YES</strong></td>
    <td><strong>YES</strong></td>
    <td><strong>NO (wiped every render)</strong></td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Best Use-Case</strong></td>
    <td>UI data (seen on screen)</td>
    <td>DOM nodes, Timer IDs, Previous state</td>
    <td>Temporary local math inside function</td>
  </tr>
</table>

<div class="script-box">
  <strong>🗣️ Speaking Script:</strong><br>
  "Sir, <span class="code">useRef</span> returns a mutable object <span class="code">{ current: initialValue }</span> that persists across renders. It has two primary use cases: First, to <strong>directly reference and manipulate DOM elements</strong>, like triggering focus or managing scroll positions. Second, to <strong>hold mutable values across renders without triggering a re-render</strong> when updated—such as storing timer IDs (<span class="code">setInterval</span>), tracking previous state, or maintaining instance variables."
</div>

<h2>5. Performance Optimization: The Holy Trinity (React.memo, useMemo, useCallback)</h2>
<div class="concept-box">
  <strong>🧠 Dimag Me Samajhne Ke Liye:</strong>
  Parent re-renders cause all children to re-render. <span class="code">React.memo</span> stops child re-renders if props don't change. But because functions in JS recreate on every render (<span class="code">()=&gt;{} !== ()=&gt;{}</span>), we need <span class="code">useCallback</span> to freeze function references, and <span class="code">useMemo</span> to cache expensive calculation results.
</div>

<h3>Master Comparison Table</h3>
<table>
  <tr>
    <th>Tool</th>
    <th>What does it memoize?</th>
    <th>Where is it used?</th>
    <th>Purpose</th>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>React.memo</strong></td>
    <td>Entire <strong>Component</strong></td>
    <td>Wraps Component definition</td>
    <td>Prevents child re-render if props are shallowly equal</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>useCallback</strong></td>
    <td><strong>Function Definition</strong></td>
    <td>Inside Component</td>
    <td>Maintains stable function reference across renders</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>useMemo</strong></td>
    <td><strong>Computed Value (Result)</strong></td>
    <td>Inside Component</td>
    <td>Avoids running heavy calculations on every render</td>
  </tr>
</table>

<div class="script-box">
  <strong>🗣️ Speaking Script:</strong><br>
  "Sir, these three tools optimize rendering performance at different levels:<br>
  1. <strong>React.memo</strong> is a Higher-Order Component that memoizes an entire component's render output, skipping re-renders if incoming props are shallowly equal.<br>
  2. <strong>useCallback</strong> is a hook that memoizes the <strong>function definition itself</strong>, ensuring stable function references across re-renders so that memoized child components don't re-render unnecessarily.<br>
  3. <strong>useMemo</strong> is a hook that memoizes the <strong>result of an expensive computation</strong>, recalculating it only when its specified dependencies change."
</div>

<div class="trap-box">
  <strong>⚠️ Critical Trap: Should we memoize everything?</strong><br>
  <strong>Counter-Answer:</strong> "No! Over-memoization adds memory overhead and unnecessary comparison cost on every render. Memoize only when calculations are genuinely expensive or functions are passed to memoized children."
</div>

<h2>6. useEffect vs useLayoutEffect (PDF Q11)</h2>
<div class="script-box">
  <strong>🗣️ Speaking Script:</strong><br>
  "Sir, both hooks handle side effects, but their execution timing is fundamentally different: useEffect runs asynchronously <strong>after</strong> the browser has painted the screen, which ensures that network calls or timers don't block the UI rendering pipeline. In contrast, useLayoutEffect runs synchronously <strong>before</strong> the browser paints. It is specifically designed for DOM measurements and styling computations to prevent visual screen flickering before the user sees the element."
</div>

<h2>7. Production Search Bar: The 4-Guard Architecture</h2>
<div class="script-box">
  <strong>🗣️ Speaking Script:</strong><br>
  "To build an enterprise production-grade search bar, I implement a 4-Guard Architecture:<br>
  1. <strong>Debouncing</strong> (300ms) to throttle rapid typing bursts.<br>
  2. <strong>Minimum-Length Guard</strong> (length &gt;= 3) to eliminate redundant queries on 1-2 characters.<br>
  3. In-memory <strong>Search Cache</strong> object to resolve repeat queries in 0ms without hitting the network.<br>
  4. <strong>AbortController</strong> inside useEffect cleanup to cancel stale in-flight requests and prevent race condition overwrites."
</div>

<h2>8. Asynchronous Data Fetching & Shimmer UI (PDF Q36 & Q40)</h2>
<div class="script-box">
  <strong>🗣️ Speaking Script:</strong><br>
  "For resilient data fetching, I maintain three distinct states: data, isLoading, and error. The API call is triggered inside useEffect with an empty dependency array. I always resolve loading state using .finally(() => setIsLoading(false)) so that loading terminates cleanly on both success and rejection. In the UI, we implement Conditional Rendering: first verifying error boundaries, then presenting a Shimmer Skeleton for superior perceived performance over traditional spinners, and finally rendering the resolved card list."
</div>

<h2>9. Virtual DOM, Reconciliation & Diffing Algorithm (PDF Q46, Q47, Q48)</h2>
<div class="concept-box">
  <strong>🧠 Dimag Me Samajhne Ke Liye:</strong><br>
  1. <strong>Real DOM kyu slow hai?</strong> Real DOM direct chhedne par browser ko <strong>Reflow (Layout)</strong> aur <strong>Repaint</strong> karna padta hai jo sabse expensive hai.<br>
  2. <strong>Virtual DOM:</strong> Real DOM ka lightweight JavaScript object copy jo memory me rehta hai (House ka Blueprint).<br>
  3. <strong>Reconciliation:</strong> State/Props change hone par naya Virtual DOM banta hai. React purane aur naye VDOM ko compare karke real DOM ko sync karta hai.<br>
  4. <strong>Diffing Heuristics:</strong> O(n^3) ko O(n) banane ke do assumptions: (a) Alag element type pura subtree destroy/recreate karta hai, (b) Lists me stable identity ke liye <span class="code">key</span> use hoti hai.
</div>

<div class="script-box">
  <strong>🗣️ Speaking Script:</strong><br>
  "Sir, the <strong>Virtual DOM</strong> is a lightweight in-memory JavaScript object representation of the real DOM. Manipulating the real DOM directly is expensive because it triggers browser reflow and repaint cycles.<br><br>
  When state or props change, React generates a new Virtual DOM tree. The process of computing the difference between the previous Virtual DOM tree and the new one, and then batching only the minimal set of changes to update the real DOM, is called <strong>Reconciliation</strong>.<br><br>
  React's <strong>Diffing Algorithm</strong> optimizes this comparison from standard O(n³) complexity down to O(n) by relying on two heuristic assumptions:<br>
  1. Two elements of different HTML tag types will generate completely different trees, so React replaces the entire subtree.<br>
  2. Child elements in a list are stably identified across renders using a unique <span class="code">key</span> prop."
</div>

<h2>10. The key={index} Disaster (PDF Q53)</h2>
<div class="concept-box">
  <strong>🧠 Dimag Me Samajhne Ke Liye:</strong><br>
  Agar list dynamic hai (item add, delete, sort, filter ho rahe hain) aur humne <span class="code">key={index}</span> diya hai, toh jab top par naya item aayega, index shift ho jayenge. React sochega item wahi purana hai aur purane item ka input state ya checkbox naye item par transfer kar dega. UI glitch ho jayegi!<br>
  <strong>Rule:</strong> Hamesha unique database ID (<span class="code">item.id</span>) use karo!
</div>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Scenario</th>
      <th>Kya key={index} chalega?</th>
      <th>Reason</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Static List</strong> (e.g. Nav items jo kabhi reorder/filter nahi hote)</td>
      <td>✅ <strong>SAFE</strong></td>
      <td>List ka order aur count kabhi badalta nahi hai</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Dynamic List</strong> (Add, Delete, Filter, Sort)</td>
      <td>❌ <strong>CRITICAL BUG</strong></td>
      <td>Index shift hote hi component state cross-wire ho jati hai</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>List with Inputs / Checkboxes</strong></td>
      <td>❌ <strong>CRITICAL BUG</strong></td>
      <td>Uncontrolled state galat item ko bind ho jati hai</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class="script-box">
  <strong>🗣️ Speaking Script:</strong><br>
  "Sir, using array index as a key is an anti-pattern when the list is dynamic. React uses keys to track the identity of elements across renders. If an item is inserted, deleted, or sorted, the indices shift. React then matches components based on positional index rather than true data identity, leading to severe bugs such as mismatched input states, corrupted checkboxes, broken animations, and redundant re-renders. We should strictly use unique, stable identifiers from data like <span class="code">item.id</span>."
</div>

<h2>11. React Fiber Architecture: React 15 vs React 16+ (PDF Q54, Q55)</h2>
<div class="concept-box">
  <strong>🧠 Dimag Me Samajhne Ke Liye:</strong><br>
  1. <strong>React 15 (Stack Reconciler):</strong> Synchronous aur recursive tha. Bada tree render karte waqt browser main thread block ho jata tha, frames drop (lag) hote the.<br>
  2. <strong>React 16 (Fiber Reconciler):</strong> Complete rewrite. Rendering ko chhote-chhote units ("Fibers") me todta hai.<br>
  3. <strong>Superpowers:</strong> <strong>Incremental Rendering (Time Slicing)</strong> - render ko pause karke user interaction handle kar sakta hai, fir resume karta hai. <strong>Priority Scheduling</strong> - user typing/clicking ko background data rendering se pehle run karta hai.<br>
  4. <strong>2 Phases:</strong> <em>Render Phase</em> (Asynchronous, interruptible, calculates diffs) aur <em>Commit Phase</em> (Synchronous, fast, flushes updates to Real DOM).
</div>

<div class="script-box">
  <strong>🗣️ Speaking Script:</strong><br>
  "Sir, <strong>React Fiber</strong> is the complete rewrite of React's reconciliation engine introduced in React 16 to solve the limitations of the older Stack Reconciler.<br><br>
  In React 15's Stack Reconciler, rendering was synchronous and recursive, holding the main thread hostage and causing dropped frames (jank).<br><br>
  Fiber introduced two revolutionary capabilities:<br>
  1. <strong>Incremental Rendering (Time Slicing):</strong> It breaks render work into small units called 'Fibers' that can be paused, resumed, or aborted based on browser idle deadlines.<br>
  2. <strong>Priority-Based Scheduling:</strong> High-priority user interactions (typing, clicking) take precedence over low-priority background updates (off-screen rendering).<br><br>
  Fiber operates in two distinct phases: The <strong>Render Phase</strong> (asynchronous, interruptible diff calculation) and The <strong>Commit Phase</strong> (synchronous, uninterruptible DOM mutation)."
</div>

